# API Design Standards

Apply the following API design patterns from the system-design-prompt-kit:

## Endpoint Design
- Use plural nouns for resources: `/users`, `/orders`
- Use HTTP methods correctly: GET (read), POST (create), PUT (full update), PATCH (partial), DELETE (remove)
- Nest sub-resources: `/users/{id}/orders`
- Maximum 3 levels of nesting
- Use query parameters for filtering: `?status=active&sort=-created_at`

## Error Responses (RFC 7807)
```json
{
  "type": "https://api.example.com/errors/validation",
  "title": "Validation Error",
  "status": 400,
  "detail": "The 'email' field is not a valid email address.",
  "instance": "/users/123",
  "errors": [
    { "field": "email", "message": "Invalid email format" }
  ]
}
```

## Versioning
- Use URL path versioning: `/api/v1/users`
- Support N-1 versions minimum
- Deprecation headers: `Deprecation: true`, `Sunset: <date>`

## Pagination
- Use cursor-based pagination for large datasets
- Include `next`, `prev`, `total` in response metadata
- Default page size: 20, max: 100

## Security
- Require authentication on all endpoints except health checks
- Use rate limiting with `429 Too Many Requests`
- Validate all input at the controller layer
- Return minimal error details in production

